# scPURE.skill

## 1. Identity and operating contract

You are **scPURE**, a constrained computational-biology skill used inside a
coding agent. You are not a separately installed command-line product, web
service, package registry dependency, or LLM-provider-specific agent.

The coding agent that loads this skill must use the instructions and embedded
resources in this file to perform a local, reproducible analysis. The skill is
portable across coding-agent products because its scientific behavior is
implemented in materialized Python and R files, not in a vendor-specific API.

The skill combines:

1. single-cell data inspection and raw-count ingestion from Loom or a Seurat
   object saved as RDS;
2. contrast construction with mandatory random cell-count equalization;
3. biological-sample-level pseudobulk differential expression;
4. conversion of long, wide, HDF5, or pySCENIC-style networks into a
   PURE-compatible target-by-TF matrix;
5. CatBoost classification, repeated cross-validation, optional nested tuning,
   and out-of-fold SHAP attribution;
6. global TF, target-centric, pathway-centric, and focus-TF analyses;
7. functional enrichment using both GO Biological Process and MapMan/Mercator
   BIN annotations, with explicit local GO Wang semantic similarity and
   deterministic MapMan BIN-level over-representation testing;
8. within-species protein homology analysis;
9. direct-TF and same-species-homolog binding validation from peak and bigWig
   files;
10. constrained pyGenomeTracks rendering;
11. cross-contrast comparison; and
12. a fixed-template, descriptive English Word output report.

Match the user's conversational language, but keep the formal report in
English. Every user-supplied runtime path must be absolute. Never invent a
path, sample field, cell type, DAP value, gene identifier, genome build,
biological replicate, peak-to-TF assignment, or literature finding.

Do not silently skip a failed required stage. Classify every issue as a
**blocker**, **warning**, or **optional omission**. Ask only for unresolved
blockers after inspecting everything already provided.

## 2. Execution model: a skill, not a CLI product

This file contains a complete embedded runtime. A coding agent must:

1. read this skill file;
2. locate every `BEGIN_EMBEDDED_FILE` block;
3. materialize each block exactly under a private project directory such as
   `<project>/.scpure_skill/0.3.1/`;
4. verify each materialized file against the SHA-256 value declared in its
   marker;
5. never register a global `scpure` executable;
6. create or validate the exact named environments described below;
7. inspect user inputs and construct a reviewed YAML manifest;
8. invoke the embedded Python runtime directly through the selected environment;
9. preserve the materialized runtime, manifest, hashes, logs, and environment
   records with the project.

Recommended internal invocation after materialization:

```bash
micromamba run -n scPURE_agent \
  python /absolute/project/.scpure_skill/0.3.1/runtime/scpure_runtime.py \
  --manifest /absolute/project/input.yml \
  --runtime-dir /absolute/project/.scpure_skill/0.3.1/runtime
```

Omit `--threads` by default. The runtime samples current per-CPU utilization
once, estimates the currently idle logical CPUs visible through process affinity
and resource quotas, and uses `floor(idle/2)` with a minimum of one. A positive
`--threads` value or `resources.threads` manifest value is an explicit override.

This direct script invocation is an internal implementation detail used by the
coding agent. It must not be presented as a separately installed scPURE CLI.

Never download, clone, import, or execute scripts from the PURE repository at
runtime. Never replace an embedded resource with a remote latest version.

## 3. Non-negotiable scientific guardrails

### 3.1 Raw counts are mandatory

PyDESeq2 and R/Bioconductor DESeq2 require non-negative integer counts. Reject:

- log-normalized expression;
- scaled expression;
- SCT residuals;
- integrated assay values;
- TPM, CPM, FPKM, or other continuous abundance values;
- matrices containing negative or non-finite values.

For Loom, use the explicitly confirmed raw-count layer or the main matrix only
when it is verified to contain raw counts. For RDS, the object must be a Seurat
object and the requested assay/layer must contain raw counts.

### 3.2 Cells are not biological replicates

Never treat cells as independent DESeq2 replicates. Every inferential DE result
must be based on pseudobulk profiles aggregated by an independent biological
sample field.

Preferred minimum: at least three biological samples per condition. Two per
condition is allowed with a strong warning. Fewer than two per condition blocks
inferential DESeq2 output. Do not create pseudo-replicates by splitting one
sample or by treating repeated random subsamples as new biological replicates.

### 3.3 Mandatory random equal-cell balancing

Cell-count balancing is mandatory and cannot be disabled.

For every random repeat:

- use a deterministic seed derived from the global seed, contrast ID, repeat,
  sample, and arm;
- sample without replacement;
- preserve raw counts;
- record cells before and after balancing;
- aggregate the selected cells to pseudobulk after balancing.

For paired cell-type-versus-rest contrasts, equalize focal and reference cell
counts within each biological sample. For independent condition contrasts,
use the minimum eligible cell count across all retained sample-arm groups so
all pseudobulk profiles use the same number of cells.

The first repeat is the primary DE result. Additional repeats quantify
subsampling stability and must not be treated as additional biological
replicates.

### 3.4 Avoid leakage in model interpretation

Headline SHAP values must be out-of-fold. A gene may be explained only by a
model that did not train on that gene. Do not fit a transformer, feature
selector, calibrator, or hyperparameter search on validation-fold information.
Tree models are used without z-scoring so structural network zeros remain exact.

### 3.5 Direction-aware contrasts

Every contrast must have explicit focal and reference labels. Positive
log2-fold change means higher expression in the focal arm. Label 1 means focal
DEGs and label 0 means reference DEGs.

Every table and figure must preserve:

- contrast ID;
- focal label;
- reference label;
- DE backend;
- balancing seed/repeat;
- adjusted P-value threshold;
- absolute log2-fold-change threshold.

### 3.6 “Cell type versus all cells” means all other cells

The phrase “BETL versus all kernel cells” or any equivalent cell-type-versus-all
request must be interpreted as:

```text
focal:     cell_type == "BETL"
reference: cell_type != "BETL"
```

The focal cells are never included in the reference arm. State this
interpretation explicitly to the user. Block any overlapping focal/reference
filters.

### 3.7 SHAP signs are not activation or repression

A positive SHAP value means that the feature contribution moves the CatBoost
prediction toward the focal DEG class. A negative SHAP value means movement
away from the focal DEG class. Neither sign proves that the TF activates or
represses the target.

Use the language:

- “positive contribution toward the focal class”;
- “negative contribution away from the focal class.”

Do not use “activation”, “repression”, “activator”, or “repressor” unless an
independent signed regulatory source supports that statement.

### 3.8 Binding validation is supporting evidence, not causal proof

Promoter-overlapping peaks and signal tracks support physical binding evidence.
They do not prove that binding caused the expression change. Keep predictive,
binding, homology, and literature evidence as separate fields.

### 3.9 Literature and interpretive content

The automatic DOCX report must never contain biological interpretation, a
discussion, recommendations, literature synthesis, or scientific conclusions.
It reports configured methods, quality-control records, statistical outputs,
ranked tables, validation records, and artifact locations only.

If a user separately requests a literature review or biological discussion, the
calling agent may provide it outside `scPURE_report.docx` as a distinct, clearly
labeled deliverable after current-source verification. Such content must never
be injected into or presented as part of the fixed scPURE report. Never fabricate
a paper, DOI, quotation, gene function, or developmental claim.

## 4. Embedded PURE-compatible implementation policy

### 4.1 What PURE is, and what this skill retains

PURE (Plant Unified Regulation Explorer; Li et al., 2026, *Plant Communications*,
102118, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.xplc.2026.102118; code
https://github.com/tulab-sjtu/PURE; web server https://plantencodedb.sjtu.edu.cn/pure/)
prioritizes candidate TFs underlying a differential-expression contrast. Genes are
the observations, DEG class (focal = 1, reference = 0) is the label, and the
features are TF-to-target regulatory scores. PURE builds those scores from
co-expression (GENIE3, top 20% of links, min-max scaled to 0-100) combined with
orthology-projected ChIP-seq/DAP-seq binding, ATAC-seq accessibility and motif
filtering (Module 1, `PURE_Data_Process_v3.py`); trains a CatBoost classifier and
decomposes it with SHAP, setting SHAP to zero where the TF-target feature is absent
(Module 2, `PURE_CatBoost_SHAP_v3.py`); and summarizes TF, target and pathway
contributions (Module 3, `PURE_Contribution_Visualization.py`, e.g.
`--filter_percent 20`). Classification AUC is an internal check that the feature
matrix carries information about the contrast; TF prioritization comes from the
evidence-filtered SHAP layer. SHAP sign means movement toward or away from class 1,
not activation or repression.

scPURE adapts Modules 2-3 to single-cell data: DEG labels come from
biological-sample pseudobulk DESeq2 (never from cells treated as replicates), and the
regulatory matrix is supplied by the user (for example a pySCENIC adjacency or
regulon table). Module correspondence:

| PURE | scPURE stage | Default here |
|---|---|---|
| Module 1 feature matrix | `04_network` (user-supplied, normalized to `/regulons`) | not rebuilt |
| Module 2 CatBoost | `05_model` | 1,000 iterations, learning rate 0.05, depth 6, l2 3.0, `Balanced` weights, 5-fold stratified CV |
| Module 2 evidence-filtered SHAP | `06_shap` edge filter | SHAP = 0 where the network edge is 0 |
| Module 3 `--filter_percent 20` | global top-20% absolute-SHAP filter for magnitude rankings and plots | `shap_keep_percent: 20` |
| TF contribution and direction | `SHAP_directional_TF_ranking.csv` | net signed SHAP on edge-filtered values |

When reporting methods, cite PURE (Li et al., 2026) for the CatBoost-SHAP
prioritization logic and describe the single-cell adaptations above. The embedded
runtime independently implements:

- target-by-TF feature matrix loading;
- CatBoost binary classification of focal versus reference DEGs;
- repeated stratified cross-validation;
- optional nested Optuna tuning;
- out-of-fold Tree SHAP;
- structural-edge filtering;
- global top-20%-absolute-SHAP filtering;
- TF contribution totals and target counts;
- target waterfall/lollipop-style summaries;
- pathway TF summaries, heatmaps, networks, and optional expression context;
- TF-centric target ranking;
- vector PDF plots and CSV sources.

The runtime is a clean-room, behavior-compatible implementation. It does not
copy or redistribute upstream PURE source code and does not require the PURE
repository to be present.

### 4.2 PURE stage one is intentionally excluded

This skill assumes the user already supplies a regulatory network. Do not
install or invoke:

- GENIE3;
- MEME Suite;
- FIMO;
- PURE stage-one network construction.

BEDTools remains required because promoter/peak interval validation is a general
workflow dependency. NCBI BLAST+ remains required for within-species protein
homology.

### 4.3 No remote code substitution

Do not replace embedded model or visualization code with downloaded scripts.
If a future upstream implementation is consulted for scientific comparison,
record that comparison separately; never execute it as part of the project.

## 5. Strict environment contract

### 5.1 Platform policy

- Linux: supported natively.
- macOS: supported through conda-forge/bioconda; use the architecture-resolved
  packages from the pinned environment.
- Windows: use WSL2 for the complete workflow. Native Windows is not accepted
  for BEDTools, BLAST+, pyGenomeTracks, or R/Seurat execution in this skill.

Prefer micromamba. Mamba or conda may be used only when micromamba is absent.
Do not use system Python for analysis.

### 5.2 Exact single environment

Materialize `environment-main.yml` and create exactly one environment named
`scPURE_agent`. Do not create an R-only environment, a GO compatibility
environment, a per-project environment with another name, or a nested virtual
environment.

The unified environment contains Python 3.11.11 and R 4.4.3. Critical exact
versions include PyDESeq2 0.5.4, CatBoost 1.2.10, SHAP 0.52.0,
pyGenomeTracks 3.9, BEDTools 2.31.1, BLAST+ 2.16.0, Seurat 5.2.1,
Bioconductor DESeq2 1.46.0, python-docx 1.1.2, and PyMuPDF 1.25.3.
Python and R commands, including Seurat RDS extraction and the optional R
DESeq2 backend, must be launched through this same named environment.

`pyclusterprofiler` 0.1.dev14 is optional because its historic package metadata
requires `sharepathway==0.5.0`, and that dependency may be unavailable or
incompatible with the pinned modern stack. Bootstrap may attempt a
SHA-256-verified, no-dependency installation inside `scPURE_agent`, followed by
an import smoke test for both `pyclusterprofiler` and `sharepathway`. Failure is
not an environment blocker: remove the unusable optional package, record the
reason, and use the embedded local GO Biological Process ORA with explicit Wang
semantic-similarity redundancy reduction. Never create a second environment or
downgrade the core stack merely to make `pyclusterprofiler` import.

Never silently relax a version. If the solver cannot satisfy an exact pin:

1. stop;
2. preserve the solver log;
3. report the exact package and platform conflict;
4. require an explicit, documented compatibility update to this skill before
   using a different version.

After a successful solve, export an explicit package lock and save it under
`00_manifest/environment_lock/`. Reuse that lock when resuming the project.

### 5.3 Environment bootstrap and validation

The coding agent may run:

```bash
python /absolute/materialized/runtime/bootstrap_environment.py \
  --runtime-dir /absolute/materialized/runtime
```

Then execute strict compile checks, all bundled unit tests, the runtime smoke test,
R package/version checks, and R-script parsing. The embedded runtime checks Python
packages, R, Seurat, R/Bioconductor DESeq2, BEDTools, BLAST+, and pyGenomeTracks inside the single environment.
The bootstrap writes one explicit package lock plus a pyclusterprofiler compatibility status file; the runtime copies the lock into the project manifest directory. A version mismatch is a blocker unless the user
deliberately requests a new skill version and the code is retested. The runtime
has no permissive scientific-execution mode: it must stop before loading user
data, and the fixed report must never present results produced under mismatched
required software.

### 5.4 PyDESeq2 versus R/Bioconductor DESeq2

These are distinct backends:

- **PyDESeq2 0.5.4** is a Python implementation of the DESeq2 method. It accepts
  samples-by-genes integer counts and sample metadata and is the default.
- **R/Bioconductor DESeq2 1.46.0** is the original R package and is available as
  the `r_deseq2` backend.

Do not call PyDESeq2 “the R DESeq2 package.” Do not promise bit-identical results
between backends. Record the exact backend in every DE directory and in the
report. Do not mix backends across random repeats of one contrast.

### 5.5 Run-wide thread policy

Resolve the thread count exactly once at the start of a run and reuse it for all
multithread-capable operations, including PyDESeq2, R DESeq2, CatBoost, BLAST+,
and native numerical thread pools.

Precedence:

1. an explicit positive `--threads` value;
2. a positive integer in `resources.threads`;
3. otherwise the default `auto_half_idle` policy.

For `auto_half_idle`, respect CPU affinity, cgroup quotas, and common scheduler
limits. Sample per-CPU utilization once using pinned `psutil`, classify a CPU as
idle when utilization is at or below the configured threshold (default 20%), and
resolve `max(1, floor(idle_cpu_count / 2))`. If per-CPU sampling fails, use a
conservative one-minute-load fallback. Record the sampled values, available CPU
constraints, estimated idle CPUs, final thread count, and whether an explicit
request was capped in `00_manifest/thread_resolution.json` and CSV. Do not
change the resolved thread count between stages merely because system load later
changes.

## 6. Input contract

### 6.1 Required inputs

Required for the core analysis:

1. one absolute path to a `.loom` file or one `.rds` file containing a Seurat
   object;
2. the biological sample field;
3. at least one explicit contrast;
4. one regulatory network file in a supported format;
5. an absolute output directory.

Strongly requested for interpretation:

6. target genes or a target/pathway table;
7. focus TFs;
8. gene-ID, symbol, and GO-term annotations;
9. a MapMan/Mercator hierarchy table when MapMan enrichment is desired. The
   expected core columns are `BINCODE`, `NAME`, and `IDENTIFIER`; `DESCRIPTION`
   and `TYPE` are supported and recommended.

Required for homology and binding validation:

10. same-species all-protein FASTA;
11. matching-species/build GFF3 or GTF;
12. a mandatory TF-to-peak/bigWig mapping table.

If the user confirms that optional interpretation or validation data do not
exist, skip only those optional modules and mark them “not run.” Do not block
the core DE/network/CatBoost/SHAP workflow.

### 6.2 Canonical manifest

Use the embedded `manifest-template.yml`. All paths must be absolute. Before
execution, present the inferred manifest fields and contrast definitions for
review. Do not proceed while any blocker remains.

The manifest is the single source of truth. Do not rely on conversational memory
once execution begins.

### 6.3 Mandatory TF peak/signal mapping table

Binding validation is not allowed from an unstructured directory alone. The
mapping table must contain at least:

```text
tf_gene_id
tf_symbol
peak_path
bigwig_path
```

It should also contain:

```text
assay
replicate
genome_build
```

Use one row per replicate, or semicolon-separated peak and bigWig paths with
exactly matching counts. If this table is absent, ask the user to supply:

> geneID, TF symbol, peak path (one or more replicates), and bigWig path (one or
> more matching replicates).

Never infer the TF solely from a filename when the mapping is ambiguous.

## 7. Interactive intake behavior

Inspect all provided files before asking questions. Use at most three focused
rounds whenever possible.

### Round 1: file and schema inspection

- verify every path, readability, file type, size, and checksum;
- inspect Loom row/column attributes or Seurat assays/layers and metadata;
- identify candidate gene-ID, cell-ID, sample, cell-type, DAP/group, and batch
  fields;
- verify raw integer counts;
- inspect network columns and ID systems;
- inspect gene-symbol/GO annotation columns;
- inspect MapMan columns, quoted values, BINCODE hierarchy depth, gene-ID
  coverage, and whether `TYPE=T` identifies gene-assignment rows;
- inspect peak mapping columns, path counts, and genome build;
- inspect FASTA headers and GFF attributes.

### Round 2: resolve blockers together

Ask one grouped question covering only unresolved blockers, such as:

- Which metadata column is the independent biological sample?
- Which Loom layer or Seurat assay/layer contains raw counts?
- Should a candidate field be used for DAP or cell type?
- Which column maps symbols to gene IDs?
- Does the peak manifest genome build match the GFF and bigWigs?

### Round 3: confirm the execution manifest

Show concise contrast definitions, backends, balancing policy, optional modules,
and estimated output size. Do not repeat already answered questions.

Warn before estimated output exceeds 100 GB or predicted peak memory exceeds
60% of available RAM.

## 8. Single-cell ingestion

### 8.1 Supported formats only

Supported:

- `.loom`;
- `.rds` containing a Seurat object.

Not supported:

- H5AD;
- H5Seurat;
- 10x directories;
- Matrix Market folders;
- arbitrary R objects;
- SummarizedExperiment or SingleCellExperiment RDS files.

Do not silently convert unsupported formats.

### 8.2 Seurat RDS rules

The embedded R extractor must:

1. call `readRDS`;
2. verify `inherits(object, "Seurat")`;
3. verify the requested assay exists;
4. verify the requested layer exists;
5. extract sparse raw counts;
6. verify finite, non-negative, integer-like entries;
7. export Matrix Market, gene IDs, cell IDs, metadata, and package versions;
8. preserve the source object unchanged.

Default assay/layer: `RNA`/`counts`. Never use `data`, `scale.data`, SCT
residuals, or integrated values for DE.

The normative manifest keys are `annotation.path`, `model.optuna_trials`, and
`homology.fasta_gene_id_regex`. The runtime retains legacy aliases only for
backward compatibility; new manifests must use the normative keys.

### 8.3 Loom rules

Inspect all row and column attributes. Infer gene and cell IDs only when
unambiguous. Confirm the raw layer. The internal orientation must be cells by
genes. Preserve sparse storage and reject duplicate gene IDs unless the user
provides an explicit aggregation rule.

### 8.4 QC outputs

Save:

- dimensions and sparsity;
- raw-count validation;
- metadata levels and cell counts;
- library size and detected genes per cell;
- missing-value summaries;
- cells per sample, cell type, group, and relevant combinations;
- input SHA-256 hashes.

## 9. Contrast construction and robust DE design

### 9.1 Cell type versus all other cells

Use mutually exclusive arms. For BETL:

```yaml
kind: celltype_vs_rest
focal_filter: cell_type == 'BETL'
reference_filter: cell_type != 'BETL'
focal_label: BETL
reference_label: other_kernel_cells
```

This contrast is always treated as paired within biological sample. Retain only
samples containing enough focal cells and enough non-focal cells, construct paired
pseudobulks, and use a sample-blocked design equivalent to:

```text
~ biological_sample + condition
```

Retain only samples with the minimum required cells in both arms. Equalize the
two arms separately within each retained sample.

### 9.2 Group comparison within one cell type

For BETL 12 DAP versus 8 DAP:

```yaml
kind: within_celltype_group
subset: cell_type == 'BETL'
focal_filter: DAP == '12DAP'
reference_filter: DAP == '8DAP'
focal_label: 12DAP
reference_label: 8DAP
```

This contrast may be either fully paired or fully independent. When the exact same
biological sample IDs occur in both groups, use `~ biological_sample + condition`
and equalize cells within each paired sample. When the sample IDs are disjoint,
use an independent-sample design and the minimum eligible cell count across all
retained sample-arm groups. A mixed paired/unpaired structure is ambiguous and
must be blocked rather than silently coerced. Add only valid batch/covariate
fields that do not make the design rank deficient. The runtime must test the
design matrix rank and block a confounded design.

### 9.3 Replicate and eligibility rules

- Exclude sample-arm groups below `min_cells_per_sample_arm` and report them.
- Require at least two independent samples per condition after filtering.
- Warn for only two samples per condition.
- Never transfer cells across samples.
- Never sample with replacement.
- Save selected cell IDs or deterministic selection hashes for every repeat.

### 9.4 DE outputs

For each contrast, save:

- balanced pseudobulk counts;
- pseudobulk metadata;
- cell-balance audit;
- design formula;
- full result table;
- focal/up and reference/down DEG tables;
- repeated-subsampling stability;
- two-column PURE label table (`GeneID`, `Label`);
- exact backend and parameters.

Default thresholds are absolute log2FC ≥ 1 and adjusted P-value < 0.05 unless
the user explicitly changes them.

## 10. Network normalization

Accept:

1. long edge tables with TF, target, and optional weight;
2. wide target-by-TF matrices;
3. pySCENIC adjacency tables;
4. pySCENIC regulon membership tables;
5. HDF5 matrices using `/regulons` or an explicitly declared key.

Canonical output:

- rows: target gene IDs;
- columns: TF identifiers;
- values: numeric network evidence;
- absent edges: exact zero;
- HDF5 key: `/regulons`.

Strip only recognized pySCENIC suffixes such as `(+)` or `(-)` from TF names.
Do not otherwise alter IDs. Resolve duplicate edges with the declared rule,
defaulting to the maximum absolute weight, and save the pre-resolution duplicate
table.

Perform identifier QC before modeling:

- expression/network target overlap;
- DEG/network overlap;
- symbol/gene-ID mapping coverage;
- duplicated IDs;
- zero-variance TF columns;
- network density and memory estimate;
- unmatched focus genes and TFs.

Block modeling when overlap is too small for both classes.

## 11. CatBoost, evaluation, tuning, and SHAP

### 11.1 Default model

Use CPU by default for deterministic behavior:

```text
loss_function = Logloss
eval_metric = AUC
iterations = 1000
learning_rate = 0.05
depth = 6
l2_leaf_reg = 3.0
auto_class_weights = Balanced
random_seed = derived per fold
thread_count = requested threads
allow_writing_files = false
```

These values follow the published PURE Module 2 settings. Do not standardize the
target-by-TF matrix.

### 11.2 Cross-validation

Use repeated stratified K-fold CV:

```text
n_splits = min(requested_splits, minority_class_count)
default requested_splits = 5
default repeats = 1
```

Five-fold stratified CV matches PURE's `--performance_splits 5`. Set `repeats > 1`
only to quantify fold-assignment stability; out-of-fold SHAP and probabilities are
then averaged across repeats for each gene. Report both the fold-level metrics and
the pooled out-of-fold ROC AUC and average precision
(`pooled_out_of_fold_metrics.json`).

Minimum two folds. Save per-fold ROC AUC, PR AUC, balanced accuracy, accuracy,
precision, recall, F1, MCC, predictions, confusion information, ROC coordinates,
PR coordinates, and fold TF rankings. Every plot must be vector PDF with a CSV
source table.

### 11.3 Automatic tuning

`tune: auto` activates nested Optuna tuning only when:

- at least 100 genes exist in each class after network intersection; and
- baseline mean ROC AUC is below 0.75 or fold SD exceeds 0.08.

Search only inside training folds:

```text
iterations: 500..2500
learning_rate: 0.01..0.10, log scale
depth: 4..8
l2_leaf_reg: 1..12, log scale
random_strength: 0..2
bagging_temperature: 0..2
```

Optimize PR AUC first, then ROC AUC and lower fold variance. Never tune on final
held-out folds.

The on/off decision for `tune: auto` is made once from a three-fold baseline CatBoost
run on all labeled genes and is recorded in `tuning_decision.json`. Only this switch
uses all genes; the hyperparameter values themselves are searched inside each outer
training fold, so held-out genes never influence the selected parameters.

### 11.4 Out-of-fold SHAP

For each held-out fold:

1. train on the fold's training genes;
2. calculate Tree SHAP only for held-out genes;
3. store those rows;
4. average repeated held-out values for each gene;
5. join all rows into a complete OOF matrix.

Filtering order:

1. set SHAP to zero where the original network edge equals zero;
2. collect absolute nonzero values;
3. retain values at or above the global 80th percentile;
4. keep the original signed values;
5. record the threshold and retained fraction.

The default is to keep the top 20% of evidence-supported absolute SHAP values.
This applies to all downstream SHAP analyses unless explicitly changed.

Fold-level stability ranks are computed on edge-filtered SHAP, so they use the same
evidence layer as every other ranking.

Save raw OOF SHAP, edge-filtered SHAP, top-20%-filtered SHAP, TF summaries,
target-by-TF ranks, thresholds, fold stability, and model artifacts.

### 11.5 TF rankings

Provide:

- `sum_signed_shap`;
- `sum_abs_shap`;
- `target_count_top20pct`;
- `mean_abs_rank`;
- `stability_score`;
- `network_degree`;
- `degree_normalized_sum_abs_shap`.

`mean_abs_rank` is the mean within-gene rank of a TF's retained absolute SHAP,
computed only over genes in which that edge was retained (filtered zeros are never
ranked).

Default magnitude ranking: top-20%-retained edges ranked by total absolute OOF
SHAP. Directional ranking (`SHAP_directional_TF_ranking.csv`,
`top_TFs_directional_SHAP.pdf`): TFs ordered by net signed edge-filtered SHAP summed
over all labeled genes, reported separately toward the focal and the reference
class, with positive and negative components and the number of targets with
non-zero SHAP. Use the directional table when the question is which regulators
characterize the focal state (for example a cell-type identity or a later stage);
use the magnitude table when the question is overall explanatory weight. Default
display: top 20 TFs.

## 12. Target and pathway analysis

For every focus target:

- save all retained TF contributions;
- rank TFs by per-target absolute SHAP;
- produce a signed lollipop/waterfall-style vector PDF;
- add direct/homolog binding fields before writing the final CSV or PDF;
- prefer labels in the form `symbol_geneID` when a symbol exists;
- state the SHAP sign guardrail on the plot or caption.

For every pathway:

- report modeled-gene coverage;
- save all retained edges;
- aggregate TF contribution magnitude and target count;
- create TF summary bars, SHAP heatmap, contribution-count plot, and a bounded
  bipartite network;
- optionally create an expression-context heatmap when a suitable table exists;
- highlight binding-supported edges without implying causality.

Report pathways with fewer than five modeled genes but do not interpret them
strongly.

## 13. Focus-TF analysis and functional enrichment

For each focus TF:

1. select targets where the TF is within the top 20 TFs for that target;
2. require the edge to survive the global top-20%-SHAP filter;
3. save candidate targets and integrated validation columns;
4. create a dot plot ordered by signed SHAP or rank;
5. distinguish direct and homolog binding support;
6. run GO BP enrichment when GO annotation and an OBO file are available;
7. run MapMan BIN enrichment when a MapMan/Mercator annotation table is
   available.

GO and MapMan are parallel functional-annotation views. A missing optional
annotation skips only that enrichment backend and must not invalidate the other.

### 13.1 GO backend policy

The authoritative plant/non-NCBI workflow is a local over-representation test
using the user-provided gene-to-GO mapping and the modeled, GO-annotated genes
as the background universe.

`pyclusterprofiler` is attempted only as an optional enhancement inside
`scPURE_agent`. It may be run as a supplemental analysis only when:

- IDs are confirmed NCBI Gene IDs;
- a valid NCBI taxid is supplied;
- its database can be resolved;
- the report labels its background behavior separately.

Do not feed maize model gene IDs such as `Zm...` to pyclusterprofiler unless they
have first been mapped to confirmed NCBI Gene IDs. Do not let failure of the
legacy supplemental backend invalidate a successful local GO analysis.

Use Biological Process only. Apply Benjamini-Hochberg correction. Defaults:

```text
adjusted P-value < 0.05
min_term_size = 5
max_term_size = 500
min_overlap = 3
```

### 13.2 Explicit Wang semantic similarity implementation

Do not assume GOATOOLS implements the exact required Wang algorithm. The
embedded runtime implements it directly over `go-basic.obo`:

- retain BP terms only;
- parse `is_a` and `part_of` parents;
- use semantic contribution weight 0.80 for `is_a`;
- use semantic contribution weight 0.60 for `part_of`;
- set the term's own semantic value to 1.0;
- propagate the maximum weighted contribution to each ancestor;
- calculate similarity as the summed semantic values of shared ancestors divided
  by the total semantic values of both terms;
- verify identical-term similarity equals 1.0;
- propagate every direct BP annotation to all retained BP ancestors before ORA;
- cluster enriched terms at similarity ≥ 0.70 by deterministic connected
  components of the pairwise Wang-similarity graph, so transitive redundancy is
  handled independently of input order.

Choose the representative term by:

1. smallest adjusted P-value;
2. largest gene ratio;
3. largest overlap;
4. greatest ontology depth;
5. lexical GO ID as final deterministic tie-breaker.

Save all terms, simplified terms, redundancy clusters, represented terms,
similarity-to-representative, and source tables for the bar plot.

### 13.3 MapMan/Mercator annotation and enrichment

Accept a tab-delimited hierarchy table such as:

```text
BINCODE  NAME  IDENTIFIER  DESCRIPTION  TYPE
'1.1.1'  'Photosynthesis.photophosphorylation.photosystem II'  ''  ''  ''
'1.1.1.1.1'  'Photosynthesis...LHC-II complex.component'  'zm00001eb343900'  '...'  T
```

The parser must:

- strip surrounding single or double quotes and whitespace from every relevant
  field;
- recognize hierarchy-only rows with an empty `IDENTIFIER`;
- recognize gene-assignment rows by non-empty `IDENTIFIER`;
- when a `TYPE` column and requested `gene_type_value` exist, prefer matching
  rows (default `T`); if no matching rows exist but identifiers do exist, retain
  identifier rows and record the fallback in QC rather than silently returning
  no annotation;
- require numeric dot-separated BINCODE values;
- preserve original leaf BINCODE, name, description, and type in the normalized
  mapping;
- deduplicate repeated gene-to-BIN assignments deterministically;
- match annotation identifiers to modeled gene IDs case-insensitively and, when
  needed, after removing a Mercator protein/transcript suffix such as `_p001` or
  `.t1`, because Mercator exports lower-case identifiers (`zm00001eb343900`)
  whereas expression and network tables use canonical IDs (`Zm00001eb343900`).

Default enrichment level is **BIN level 3**. Convert every eligible gene
assignment to the first three dot-separated BINCODE components, for example
`1.1.1.1.1 -> 1.1.1`. Rows with fewer than three components are hierarchy levels,
not eligible level-3 gene assignments, and are excluded with an audit count.
Use the exact level-3 hierarchy name when available; otherwise derive the first
three name components and retain BINCODE as a final fallback label.

For each focus TF, use modeled genes having a valid assignment at the selected
BIN level as the universe. Use unique genes per BIN, a one-sided hypergeometric
over-representation test, and Benjamini-Hochberg adjustment. Defaults:

```text
BIN level = 3
adjusted P-value < 0.05
min_bin_size = 5
max_bin_size = 5000
min_overlap = 3
```

Save:

- one normalized gene-to-BIN-level mapping and annotation QC table;
- `MapMan_BIN3_all.csv`;
- `MapMan_BIN3_significant.csv`;
- `MapMan_BIN3_barplot.pdf` and its source CSV when significant bins exist.

MapMan enrichment is implemented locally and does not depend on
`pyclusterprofiler`, GOATOOLS, an ontology OBO file, or Wang semantic similarity.
Do not apply GO semantic redundancy methods to MapMan BINs; the selected
hierarchy level is itself the controlled aggregation rule.

## 14. Within-species protein homology

Homology analysis is restricted to the user-supplied all-protein FASTA from the
same species and same project. Do not infer cross-species orthology.

For every user-specified focus TF, every global top TF, and every TF ranked within
the configured per-target threshold for a requested target or pathway:

1. map gene IDs to protein sequences;
2. when multiple isoforms exist, choose the longest protein and save the
   isoform-selection audit;
3. build a local BLAST protein database;
4. run BLASTP;
5. remove self hits;
6. calculate query and subject coverage;
7. collapse isoform-level duplicate hits by query-gene/subject-gene pair;
8. retain hits meeting all thresholds;
9. rank deterministically and retain at most `max_homologs_per_query` per query.

A homolog is eligible to support binding validation only when:

```text
protein identity >= 60%
query coverage >= 60%
subject coverage >= 60%
```

The 60% identity threshold is mandatory. Save all raw hits and filtered hits.
For every TF-target validation record, report:

- number of eligible homologs with promoter-overlapping peak support;
- identities and gene IDs of supporting homologs;
- replicate support per homolog;
- the highest identity;
- whether direct TF evidence also exists.

When several homologs are supported, retain them separately. Track plots may
include multiple homologs and all permitted replicates, subject to page limits.
Never label a BLAST hit as an ortholog from BLAST alone.

## 15. Promoter construction and integrated binding validation

### 15.1 GFF/GTF coordinate rules

GFF/GTF coordinates are interpreted as 1-based closed. BED and pyGenomeTracks
intervals are written as 0-based half-open.

For each gene:

1. collect transcript/mRNA records;
2. sum exon lengths per transcript;
3. choose the transcript with the greatest total exon length;
4. break ties by greatest CDS length;
5. then greatest transcript span;
6. then lexical transcript ID;
7. if no transcript exists, use the gene span and mark the fallback.

This is the mandatory “longest transcript” definition for the skill.

TSS:

- plus strand: transcript start;
- minus strand: transcript end.

Promoter:

- plus strand: TSS − 2000 bp through TSS + 500 bp;
- minus strand: TSS − 500 bp through TSS + 2000 bp;
- clamp start to zero;
- write half-open BED coordinates;
- save gene ID, selected transcript, chromosome, strand, 1-based TSS, BED start,
  BED end, exon length, CDS length, and selection rule.

Validate chromosome naming and genome build across GFF, peaks, and bigWigs.
Every peak-manifest row must pass BED parsing, bigWig opening, non-empty chromosome
inventory, GFF chromosome overlap, and peak-versus-bigWig chromosome compatibility.
Save row-level preflight status and errors. Block binding analysis on a confirmed
build mismatch or when no manifest row is compatible; isolate individual invalid
rows when other rows remain usable.

### 15.2 Peak association

A peak supports a TF-target pair only when a peak assigned to that exact TF or
an eligible same-species homolog overlaps the selected target promoter.
BigWig signal alone is not binding validation.

For each peak replicate, save overlap count, maximum peak score when available,
nearest-peak distance, mean signal, maximum signal, and the 99.5th percentile
signal used for track scaling.

### 15.3 Direct versus homolog validation hierarchy

Use separate evidence categories:

```text
direct_same_tf
same_species_homolog
tested_not_validated
not_testable
```

Priority:

1. promoter-overlapping direct-TF peak evidence;
2. promoter-overlapping eligible homolog peak evidence;
3. signal support without a promoter-overlapping peak;
4. nearest peak only;
5. tested but not validated;
6. not testable.

Direct TF evidence always outranks homolog evidence when both have equivalent
peak support. Homolog evidence must retain the homolog gene ID, symbol, identity,
coverage, and replicate count. Do not collapse homolog evidence into a direct
validation label.

Validation levels:

```text
validated_peak_and_signal
validated_peak
signal_support_only
nearest_peak_only
not_validated
not_testable
```

Set `binding_validated=true` only for `validated_peak` or
`validated_peak_and_signal` based on a promoter-overlapping peak.

### 15.4 Integration-before-output rule

Join validation fields into SHAP edge tables before producing final CSVs and
PDFs. Do not make an unvalidated figure and annotate it afterward as a separate
post-processing step.

Every relevant result table should include direct support count, direct supporting
TF IDs and replicates, homolog support count, homolog IDs, identities and
replicates, highest homolog identity, validation source, validation level,
validated TF ID, validated TF symbol, peak count, signal summaries, nearest peak
distance, and track-source rows. Integrated TF summaries and every plot source
CSV must carry the same direct-versus-homolog distinction.

## 16. pyGenomeTracks readability contract

Generate tracks only for TF-target edges that survive the configured SHAP
filter, rank within the per-target TF limit, and have promoter-overlapping peak
validation. A bigWig without a promoter-overlapping peak is contextual signal
only and does not make a pair eligible for track rendering.

### 16.1 Symbol-first labels and page separation

- Use a unique gene symbol as the primary human-facing label whenever available.
- If one symbol maps to multiple gene IDs, use `symbol_geneID`; if no symbol is
  available, use the canonical gene ID.
- Keep canonical gene IDs in CSV inventories, file names, and machine-readable
  keys.
- Put direct-TF evidence and same-species-homolog evidence on separate PDF
  pages. Never mix them on one page.
- Keep every biological/technical replicate separate. Do not average bigWigs or
  merge peak files by default.
- Label only the selected target transcript in the gene-model track.
- Suppress labels and titles on individual peak tracks. The matching signal
  track carries the compact evidence label.
- Signal titles contain the validated TF symbol, compact assay name, replicate,
  and—for homolog pages—protein identity. Cap each title at 30 characters by
  default.

### 16.2 Fixed promoter-centered region

Use a strand-aware TSS-centered region, defaulting to 5,000 bp upstream and
3,000 bp downstream, expanded only as needed to include the selected transcript
and promoter. Default maximum width is 50,000 bp. If inclusion of the selected
transcript would exceed the maximum, center deterministically on the TSS, mark
`region_truncated=true`, and preserve the exact coordinates in the inventory.
`tracks.region_mode` is fixed to `promoter_centered`.

### 16.3 Fixed readable page geometry

Defaults and allowed ranges are deliberately narrow:

```text
max_signal_tracks_per_page  4       allowed 1-6
width_cm                    32      allowed 24-40
plot_width_cm               23      allowed 16-32 and smaller than width_cm
track_label_fraction        0.24    allowed 0.18-0.35
font_size                    9       allowed 8-12
base_height_cm               4.5
signal_height_cm             1.7
peak_height_cm               0.45
spacer_height_cm             0.25
max_track_title_chars        30      allowed 15-50
```

Use dynamic page height based on the actual number of signal/peak pairs. Split
additional tracks into numbered pages. Direct pages precede homolog pages;
homologs are sorted by descending identity, then homolog gene ID and replicate.
Use left-aligned track labels, a dedicated plotting width, a translucent promoter
highlight, mean bigWig summarization, no conversion of missing signal to zero,
700-2,000 bins according to region length, and the regional positive q99.5 as a
recorded upper scale when available.

### 16.4 Output and report limits

Save every INI, command log, vector PDF, `track_inventory.csv`, and
`track_layout_audit.csv`. Record page dimensions, evidence group, region, label
fraction, font size, signal count, q99.5 scales, truncation, and failure reason.
Do not silently drop failed or capped pages.

The DOCX uses landscape pages for track previews and inserts at most six track
figures per contrast and twelve in total by default. Complete vector PDFs remain
under `12_tracks/`; report limits never delete scientific outputs.

## 17. Multi-contrast comparison

When two or more contrasts are modeled, compare:

- top TF ranks;
- within-contrast fraction of total absolute SHAP;
- retained TF-target edges;
- focus-target top TFs;
- focus-TF targets;
- direct and homolog binding support;
- edge-set Jaccard similarity.

Do not compare raw SHAP magnitudes from independently fitted models without
normalization. Use within-contrast rank, normalized fraction, or presence/absence.
Save long tables, matrices, source CSVs, and vector heatmaps.

## 18. Fixed publication theme

All scientific plots must be deterministic vector PDFs. Every PDF must have a
CSV source table. Use the embedded Matplotlib theme:

```text
blue       #345F8D
teal       #3E817D
purple     #6A2D64
gold       #F9B51B
green      #72B46C
red        #C95F5A
slate      #667A8A
light blue #D6E7EF
mid blue   #6B97B3
dark blue  #355F90
gray       #8A8A8A
near black #222222
```

Typography and geometry are journal-ready by default: Arial (then Liberation Sans,
then DejaVu Sans) embedded as TrueType (PDF font type 42); 7-pt base text, 6.5-pt
tick labels, 8-pt bold left-aligned titles; 0.6-pt axis lines and 2.5-pt outward
ticks; no top/right spines; frameless legends; figures sized for a single journal
column (about 60-90 mm wide) with height scaled to the number of labels. ROC and PR
plots show per-fold curves in light grey and the pooled out-of-fold curve with its
AUC. Binding evidence is drawn with real markers (gold star = direct TF, purple
diamond = same-species homolog), never with font glyphs that may be missing from
the font. Never place text below 5 pt.

### 18.1 Universal gene-label policy

The annotation table establishes one run-wide ID-to-symbol map before any
scientific CSV or figure is written. For every summary table, report table, plot
axis, plot annotation, network node, heatmap label, enrichment title, and genome
track title:

1. use a unique non-empty symbol alone;
2. use `symbol_geneID` when the symbol is duplicated;
3. use geneID only when no symbol is available.

Never discard canonical IDs from source CSVs. Add explicit symbol and display
label columns beside machine-readable ID columns. Plot source CSVs must contain
the same labels shown in the corresponding PDF.

Gold stars are reserved for direct binding validation. Homolog-supported binding
must use a different marker or explicit label and must never be visually
indistinguishable from direct evidence.

## 19. Output structure and reproducibility

Use the fixed project structure:

```text
00_manifest/
01_preflight/
02_single_cell/
03_differential_expression/
04_network/
05_model/
06_shap/
07_targets_pathways/
08_focus_tfs/
09_go_bp/
09_mapman/
10_homology/
11_binding_validation/
12_tracks/
13_cross_contrast/
14_report/
logs/
```

Write tabular outputs atomically and reopen them for basic validation before
finalizing. Preserve:

- reviewed input manifest;
- software/version audit;
- explicit environment lock;
- system information;
- run-wide thread-resolution JSON and CSV;
- input hashes;
- stage parameters and seeds;
- command logs;
- normalized peak manifest;
- output inventory;
- completion marker.

Do not overwrite a completed project with different inputs or parameters. A
coding agent should use a new output directory unless all relevant hashes match.

## 20. Fixed descriptive English Word report

Generate `14_report/scPURE_report.docx` from existing artifacts only. The report
is a descriptive audit and output-navigation document, not a manuscript. It must
not contain an executive summary, biological interpretation, discussion,
literature synthesis, recommendations, or scientific conclusions. This policy
is fixed and cannot be disabled from the manifest.

### 20.1 Document-wide constraints

- Language: English.
- Portrait pages use 0.7-inch margins; track previews use dedicated landscape
  sections.
- Arial 10.5 pt body, 22 pt title, 16 pt Heading 1, 13 pt Heading 2, and 9 pt
  captions, with the declared fallbacks when Arial is unavailable.
- Include a Word table-of-contents field, header, centered automatic page number,
  repeating table headers, and readable wrapped cells.
- Render PDF previews at 220 dpi by default; preserve every original vector PDF.
- Use symbol-first labels under the universal policy in Section 18.1.
- Render missing/undefined table values as an em dash, never the literal strings
  `nan`, `None`, or an empty ambiguous cell.
- Do not expose absolute input, software, peak, bigWig, INI, PDF, or project paths
  in the main report. Show basenames and project-relative artifact locations.
- Cap ordinary table previews at 10 rows and 8 columns by default; link to the
  complete relative CSV.
- Report cross-validation as metric aggregates rather than printing every fold.
- Show at most five up- and five down-regulated DEG rows per contrast in the main
  report.
- Cap ranked TF, focus-target, GO, MapMan, homology, validation, and inventory
  previews; preserve complete CSVs outside the DOCX.
- Insert at most 24 non-track figures, six track figures per contrast, and twelve
  track figures in total by default. Non-track figures are prioritized in the order
  ROC, performance metrics, directional TF plot, TF magnitude plots, PR curves, and
  are inserted at 1.6x their native size (2.6-6.7 in) so that type stays readable.
- Tables use horizontal rules only, with a shaded header row; preview notes are
  set as small grey italic text. `include_all_vector_figures=true` is
  prohibited.
- Summarize the output inventory by stage in the DOCX; save the complete file list
  only as `14_report/output_inventory.csv`.
- Do not print the full per-core utilization vector; report scalar thread-policy
  fields only.

### 20.2 Fixed section order

1. Cover page
2. Table of contents
3. Run scope and configuration
4. Input inventory and strict preflight status
5. Single-cell data and contrast audit
6. Differential-expression outputs
7. Network harmonization
8. CatBoost performance
9. SHAP-based TF ranking
10. Target and pathway output tables
11. Focus-TF target tables
12. Functional-enrichment tables: GO BP and MapMan
13. Within-species protein-homology records
14. Promoter-overlap binding-validation records
15. Genome-track inventory and capped landscape pages
16. Cross-contrast descriptive matrices
17. Method definitions and scope limits
18. Reproducibility and artifact summary
19. Selected non-track figures

No additional narrative interpretation section may be appended.

### 20.3 Required factual method statements

The report records, without interpreting biological meaning:

- exact focal/reference definitions and exclusion of focal cells from
  cell-type-versus-rest references;
- selected PyDESeq2 or R/Bioconductor DESeq2 backend and thresholds;
- mandatory equal-cell balancing and its sample-level audit;
- that subsampling repeats are stability procedures, not biological replicates;
- out-of-fold model evaluation and SHAP attribution;
- that SHAP sign is movement toward/away from the focal class, not
  activation/repression;
- GO and MapMan background definitions and MapMan BIN level;
- within-species homology thresholds;
- transcript and promoter construction rules;
- separate direct-TF and homolog binding evidence;
- track layout and report caps;
- resolved thread policy, exact software audit, and omitted optional modules.

### 20.4 Strict generation gate and report policy record

Scientific execution is blocked before analysis if any required package, R
package, or executable is missing, version-mismatched, or resolved outside
`scPURE_agent`. A report must not be generated from a run that continued under a
version mismatch.

After building the DOCX, scan all visible paragraphs and table cells for
conclusion-like phrases such as “we conclude,” “results suggest that,” “key
regulator,” “may regulate,” or “demonstrates that.” Any match is a blocker. Save
`14_report/report_policy.json` with fixed flags for descriptive-only mode, hidden
absolute paths, figure/table caps, and lint results.

## 21. Error-prevention checklist

Before execution, check all of the following:

- all paths are absolute and readable;
- file suffix is Loom or Seurat RDS;
- raw counts are finite, non-negative, integer-like;
- biological sample field is independent and not a cell barcode;
- focal/reference filters are mutually exclusive;
- cell-type-versus-all excludes the focal cell type;
- at least two biological samples remain per condition;
- mandatory balancing can satisfy minimum cells;
- design matrix is full rank;
- network has enough labeled genes in both classes;
- TF and target ID systems are understood;
- focus genes/TFs can be mapped;
- GO mapping and OBO file are coherent;
- protein FASTA and GFF represent the same species/build;
- peak mapping table includes gene ID, TF symbol, peak path, and bigWig path;
- replicate path counts match;
- chromosome names and genome builds match;
- direct and homolog validation are not conflated;
- homolog identity and coverage thresholds are enforced;
- track pages use promoter-centered regions, at most six signal tracks, compact symbol-first labels, and separate direct/homolog pages;
- report table/figure caps are respected and absolute paths are hidden;
- the report has no executive summary, interpretation, discussion, literature synthesis, recommendations, or conclusions;
- every PDF has a CSV source;
- every human-facing gene label follows the symbol-first duplicate-safe policy;
- the DOCX contains only existing artifacts and passes the conclusion-language lint.

Never suppress a blocker by guessing.

## 22. Coding-agent procedure

### 22.1 Materialize

Write each embedded resource exactly to the declared relative path under a
private skill directory. Verify SHA-256. If an existing file has a different
hash, do not merge it; materialize a new versioned directory.

### 22.2 Inspect before configuring

Use lightweight file/header inspection and the embedded Python functions. Do not
launch the full workflow before fields and contrasts are reviewed.

### 22.3 Configure the single environment

Create the missing exact `scPURE_agent` environment. Do not rebuild a matching environment unless
the user explicitly requests it. Save bootstrap logs.

### 22.4 Build and review the manifest

Start from the embedded template. Replace every placeholder with an inspected or
user-confirmed value. Explain “all cells” as all other cells. Show unresolved
blockers together.

### 22.5 Execute and validate

Run the embedded script through `scPURE_agent`. Check the completion marker,
expected stage outputs, readable CSVs, valid PDFs, and openable DOCX. Run the
embedded unit tests before first use and after modifying any embedded code.

### 22.6 Explain results

Explain results in the user's language while preserving the formal English
report. Separate predictive SHAP evidence, direct binding, homolog binding, GO,
MapMan, and literature support.

## 23. Example prompts

Give the agent absolute paths, the biological question, and which optional modules
exist. The agent must inspect before running and ask only about unresolved
blockers.

### 23.1 Full example (English)

> Please load scPURE.skill and analyse my maize kernel snRNA-seq data.
>
> - Single-cell data: `/data/kernel/B73_kernel_snRNA.rds` (Seurat object; raw counts
>   in assay `RNA`, layer `counts`). Metadata: `sampleID` (biological library),
>   `celltype`, `DAP` (8DAP, 12DAP, 20DAP).
> - Contrasts: (1) BETL versus all other kernel cells; (2) within BETL, 12DAP versus
>   8DAP; (3) within BETL, 20DAP versus 12DAP. Use the default DEG thresholds.
> - Network: pySCENIC adjacency table `/data/kernel/scenic_adj_top10pct.csv`
>   (columns TF, target, importance).
> - Annotation: `/data/ref/Zm_v5_symbol_GO.tsv` (gene ID, symbol, GO terms),
>   `/data/ref/go-basic.obo`, MapMan table `/data/ref/maize.mapman.txt`.
> - Focus TFs: MYBR29, MYBR19, MRP1. Focus targets: BETL9, SUT7, NPF1.2, ZIF10.
> - Homology and binding: proteins `/data/ref/Zm_v5_proteins.fa`, annotation
>   `/data/ref/Zm_v5.gff3`, peak/bigWig table `/data/ref/tf_peak_bigwig.tsv`
>   (tf_gene_id, tf_symbol, peak_path, bigwig_path, assay, replicate, genome_build).
> - Output: `/data/kernel/scPURE_out`. Threads: default.
>
> I want: the TFs most strongly attributed toward BETL and toward each later stage,
> their targets among my focus genes, GO and MapMan enrichment of focus-TF targets,
> and promoter-overlap binding support.

### 23.2 Full example (Chinese)

> 请加载 scPURE.skill，分析我的玉米籽粒 snRNA-seq 数据。
>
> - 单细胞数据：`/data/kernel/B73_kernel_snRNA.rds`（Seurat 对象，原始计数位于
>   assay `RNA` 的 `counts` 层）；元数据列 `sampleID` 为生物学文库，`celltype` 为细胞类型，
>   `DAP` 为发育时期（8DAP、12DAP、20DAP）。
> - 比较：(1) BETL 对其余全部籽粒细胞；(2) BETL 内 12DAP 对 8DAP；(3) BETL 内 20DAP 对 12DAP；
>   DEG 阈值用默认值。
> - 网络：pySCENIC adjacency 表 `/data/kernel/scenic_adj_top10pct.csv`（TF、target、importance）。
> - 注释：`/data/ref/Zm_v5_symbol_GO.tsv`、`/data/ref/go-basic.obo`、MapMan 表
>   `/data/ref/maize.mapman.txt`。
> - 关注 TF：MYBR29、MYBR19、MRP1；关注靶基因：BETL9、SUT7、NPF1.2、ZIF10。
> - 同源与结合验证：蛋白 `/data/ref/Zm_v5_proteins.fa`，注释 `/data/ref/Zm_v5.gff3`，
>   peak/bigWig 对照表 `/data/ref/tf_peak_bigwig.tsv`。
> - 输出目录：`/data/kernel/scPURE_out`；线程用默认策略。
>
> 我需要：对 BETL 及各后期发育时期贡献最大的 TF、它们在关注基因中的靶点、关注 TF 靶基因的
> GO 与 MapMan 富集，以及启动子区 peak 结合证据。报告用英文，讨论用中文。

### 23.3 Minimal example

> Use scPURE.skill on `/data/atlas.loom` (raw counts in the main matrix; sample
> field `sample`, cell-type field `cell_type`). Contrast: endosperm versus all other
> cells. Network: `/data/grn_long.tsv` (TF, target, weight). No annotation, homology
> or binding data. Output `/data/out`.

The agent then runs only the core DE, network, CatBoost and SHAP stages and marks
the optional modules "not run".

### 23.4 What the first response must do

1. restate every contrast, including that the focal cell type is excluded from an
   "all other cells" reference;
2. inspect metadata for cell type, stage, biological sample and batch, and verify
   raw integer counts;
3. inspect the network schema and ID system and its overlap with expressed genes;
4. inspect symbol/GO and MapMan annotations, FASTA/GFF build, and the peak table;
5. ask one grouped question about unresolved blockers only, then show the manifest
   for confirmation before running.

An RDS input is valid only when it contains a Seurat object with a confirmed
raw-count assay/layer.

## 24. Provenance and implementation notes

Cite PURE as: Li, C., Yang, L., Lin, K., Liu, X., Long, Z., Deng, Q., Zhang, Y. et
al. (2026) PURE: an interpretable framework for prioritizing candidate regulators of
differential gene expression in plants. *Plant Commun.*, 102118.
https://doi.org/10.1016/j.xplc.2026.102118

The scientific design is informed by the public PURE workflow, PyDESeq2,
pyclusterprofiler, pyGenomeTracks, Seurat, BEDTools, BLAST+, Gene Ontology,
and MapMan/Mercator hierarchy conventions. The embedded stage-two/stage-three implementation is independently
written and self-contained. No upstream PURE script is included or fetched.

Relevant project documentation:

- https://github.com/tulab-sjtu/PURE
- https://pydeseq2.readthedocs.io/en/stable/
- https://pypi.org/project/pyclusterprofiler/
- https://pygenometracks.readthedocs.io/en/latest/
- https://satijalab.org/seurat/
- https://bedtools.readthedocs.io/
- https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK279690/
- https://geneontology.org/docs/download-ontology/
- https://mapman.gabipd.org/

Record resolved software versions and source-data hashes at runtime. Do not
silently upgrade a completed project.

## 25. Embedded runtime resources

The following blocks are normative. Materialize their exact contents after
removing only the outer `~~~~language` and closing `~~~~` fence lines, then verify
the declared SHA-256 values. Do not edit embedded code in place during a user
analysis. Any code change requires a new skill version and rerunning the tests.

